<body>
  <a class="skip-link" href="#team">Skip to team</a>
  <header class="site-header">
    <div class="header-inner">
      <div class="header-top">
        <div><p class="brand-name">The Human Loop</p><p class="tagline">Thoughtful engineering, with AI in the loop.</p></div>
        <button id="theme-toggle" type="button" aria-label="Switch to night mode">☾</button>
      </div>
      <nav class="site-nav" aria-label="Main navigation"><a href="#team">Meet the team</a><a href="#future-of-se">The future of software engineering</a></nav>
    </div>
  </header>
  <main>
    <section id="team" aria-labelledby="team-heading">
      <p class="section-kicker">Meet the people behind the work</p>
      <h1 id="team-heading">A team built around better questions.</h1>
      <p class="team-intro">We are The Human Loop, a group in the Agentic Software Engineering lab at Paderborn University. What connects us is a curiosity about how software gets made—and a shared belief that powerful AI tools need thoughtful engineers behind them. We bring different interests and experiences to one common challenge: building technology with care.</p>
      <div class="member-grid">
        <article class="member-card" data-member="Aniket"><img class="member-photo" src="{{image:aniket.jpg}}" alt="Photo of Aniket"><h3>Aniket</h3><p class="study-line">M.Sc. Computer Science, 1st semester · Paderborn University</p><p class="bio">Aniket is pursuing an M.Sc. in Computer Science at Paderborn University after earning a bachelor's degree in Computer Engineering from Savitribai Phule Pune University. He enjoys travelling and cooking, alongside a long list of creative and active pursuits. A theatre skit once earned him an award at an Asia-level national competition. Aniket sees AI as a useful assistant for coding, debugging and learning, but believes developers must understand and verify its output rather than depend on it blindly.</p><p class="hot-take">🔥 Developers who use AI effectively may replace those who don't.</p></article>
        <article class="member-card" data-member="Dheeraj"><img class="member-photo" src="{{image:dheeraj.jpg}}" alt="Photo of Dheeraj"><h3>Dheeraj</h3><p class="study-line">M.Sc. Computer Science, 1st semester · Paderborn University</p><p class="bio">Dheeraj is a first-semester M.Sc. Computer Science student at Paderborn University, following a B.Tech in Computer Science and Engineering at Anurag University. Software development, gaming and anime are among his interests, and he also makes time for fitness. He helped organise a university hackathon that drew more than 800 participants. Dheeraj values AI for speeding up development, but notes that convincing outputs can still be wrong; understanding fundamentals and critically reviewing generated solutions matter more than ever.</p><p class="hot-take">🔥 The best engineers know what code shouldn't be written.</p></article>
        <article class="member-card" data-member="Guru_akaash"><img class="member-photo" src="{{image:guru_akaash.jpg}}" alt="Photo of Guru"><h3>Guru Akaash</h3><p class="study-line">M.Sc. Computer Science, 1st semester · University of Paderborn</p><p class="bio">Guru Akaash (Guru_akaash) is pursuing an M.Sc. in Computer Science at the University of Paderborn after completing a B.E. in Computer Engineering at the University of Mumbai. Outside software development, he enjoys piano and music, table tennis, exploring new places and learning to cook. His work has supported platforms serving over 700,000 users and one million groups; he has also won national hackathons and secured a $15,000 Microsoft AI for Earth grant. He believes strong engineers use AI to amplify—not outsource—their judgment.</p><p class="hot-take">🔥 Use AI to amplify your engineering capabilities, not outsource your engineering thinking.</p></article>
        <article class="member-card" data-member="Srivallabh"><img class="member-photo" src="{{image:srivallabh.jpg}}" alt="Photo of Srivallabh"><h3>Srivallabh</h3><p class="study-line">M.Sc. Computer Science, 1st semester</p><p class="bio">Srivallabh is a first-semester M.Sc. Computer Science student with hands-on full-stack development experience. He follows Formula 1, auto journalism and cricket. One unusually memorable day, he slept for 20 hours. In his development workflow, AI has made debugging, boilerplate, codebase exploration and feature implementation noticeably faster, and he treats language models as useful collaborators for discussing solutions. He expects engineers to shift toward defining problems, designing systems and reviewing agent-produced work, while remaining accountable for the result.</p><p class="hot-take">🔥 Knowing what not to let AI do is becoming the real skill.</p></article>
        <article class="member-card" data-member="Yashas"><img class="member-photo" src="{{image:yashas.jpg}}" alt="Photo of Yashas"><h3>Yashas</h3><p class="study-line">M.Sc. Computer Science, 1st semester · Paderborn University</p><p class="bio">Yashas is studying for an M.Sc. in Computer Science after completing a B.Tech in Computer Science with a specialisation in AI and Machine Learning at Reva University. His interests include machine learning, distributed systems and coding. He also enjoys cooking food for other people. Yashas sees AI as a capable pair programmer for scaffolding and debugging, but points out that it often lacks architectural context. He believes developers still need to check generated code for security and maintainability, and sees system design as an increasingly important part of the job.</p><p class="hot-take">🔥 Prompt engineering is a temporary fad that will vanish once models understand human intent.</p></article>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section id="future-of-se" aria-labelledby="future-heading">
      <p class="section-kicker">A shared perspective</p><h2 id="future-heading">The Future of Software Engineering</h2>
      <div class="future-copy"><p>Across our team, there is a clear expectation that software engineering will become more AI-assisted—not less dependent on engineering judgment. As agents take on more routine coding, testing, debugging and even end-to-end tasks, developers will have more room to define the real problem, shape the architecture and make deliberate decisions about what should be built.</p><p>That shift makes critical review more important, not less. AI can accelerate learning and implementation, but generated code still needs human attention for correctness, security and maintainability. Creativity, accountability and a deep understanding of systems remain essential as the tools change.</p></div>
      <div class="future-points"><article class="future-point"><h3>Design the system</h3><p>Spend less time on repetition and more on architecture, constraints and the problem worth solving.</p></article><article class="future-point"><h3>Review with intent</h3><p>Understand AI-generated work, check its assumptions and own the quality of the result.</p></article><article class="future-point"><h3>Keep learning</h3><p>Use AI to extend engineering capability while keeping human judgment firmly in the loop.</p></article></div>
    </section>
  </main>
  <footer><div class="footer-inner"><p>Built by an agent, supervised by humans. 2026 · The Human Loop</p></div></footer>
